  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nCurrent satellite imagery shows a building deck of cirrostratus \nclouds that will be inhibiting chances of freezing fog. With \ntemperatures and dew points not expected to meet, albeit by 2 \ndegrees, the chance exists but is low (20% confidence). Valley \nareas, specifically, Glasgow and Wolf Point may be the only areas \nthat potentially develop freezing fog. \n\nRidging conditions continue to build to bring dry, unseasonably \nwarm temperatures to our area on Wednesday persisting into \nSaturday. A cold front is expected to pass through our area \nThursday night/Friday morning, which are causing PoPs to elevate. \nHowever, any chances of precipitation making its way to the ground\nremains low (15%-20%). This front will bring gusty conditions that\nmay warrant a Lake Wind Advisory on Friday, although we will await\nfurther model guidance before issuing any product regarding wind.\nIf traveling Thursday evening, keep in mind the potential for \nstrong and gusty west to northwest winds across central and \nsouthern Montana, which could cause dangerous crosswinds on \nroadways.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & NBM DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was mainly followed with REFS and RRFS blending included to\ndisplay the reduction in visibilities and the chance of \nfreezing fog. Deviations from prior model runs have significantly\nreduced the chances of any freezing fog Wednesday AM. Confidence \nin lack of precipitation from this cold frontal passage remains \nmoderate (45%-60%). Confidence for freezing fog to develop remains\nlow (20%). Confidence in unseasonably warmer and drier conditions\nis high (85%).\n\nFINDLEY",
